--TEST--
Forty values through a `Core\Task\Channel` at seven capacities — including the two on either side of a bound the producer never reaches — deliver one identical stream, counted rather than read off a line — ADR 0072 § 4's "nothing still running" over `nvs_stdlib::channel`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep, and the sweep is the point: capacity is the one
// thing a channel is constructed with, and every case beside this one fixes it
// at a single number. A `send` that miscounted the bound by one, a `close`
// that raced the last value out, or a queue whose key wrapped would each still
// look right at some capacity and wrong at another — so what is asserted here
// is that *no* capacity disagrees, not what any one of them answered.
class Runs {
    public static int $agreed = 0;
    public static int $disagreed = 0;
    public static int $count = 0;
    public static int $sum = 0;
    public static int $last = 0;
    public static bool $ordered = true;
}

// 1 makes every send after the first suspend; 40 and 41 sit on either side of
// the bound the producer never reaches, so at those two `send` returns forty
// times without ever parking and the whole wait is the consumer's. A channel
// that only worked when it filled fails at 41, and one that only worked when
// it did not fails at 1.
array<uint> $caps = [1, 2, 3, 5, 13, 40, 41];

foreach ($caps as uint $capacity) {
    var $chan = new Core\Task\Channel<int>($capacity);
    Runs::$count = 0;
    Runs::$sum = 0;
    Runs::$last = 0;
    Runs::$ordered = true;

    // Both halves are children of this call, so ADR 0072 § 4's promise is what
    // makes the counters final by the time the `if` below reads them: `all`
    // does not return with the producer or the consumer still running.
    Core\Task::all({
        produce: fn(): int => {
            foreach (Core\Arr::range(1, 40) as int $n) {
                $chan->send($n);
            }
            // The only end-of-stream signal there is; without it the
            // consumer's `foreach` waits forever and the group never returns.
            $chan->close();
            return 40;
        },
        consume: fn(): int => {
            foreach ($chan as int $n) {
                if ($n != Runs::$last + 1) {
                    Runs::$ordered = false;
                }
                Runs::$last = $n;
                Runs::$count += 1;
                Runs::$sum += $n;
            }
            return Runs::$count;
        },
    });

    // 40 values and 1+2+…+40 = 820. A dropped value moves both counters one
    // way and a duplicate moves them the other, so the pair pins the multiset
    // and `$ordered` pins the sequence.
    if (Runs::$count == 40 && Runs::$sum == 820 && Runs::$ordered) {
        Runs::$agreed += 1;
    } else {
        Runs::$disagreed += 1;
    }
}

// Counted, not printed per capacity: a line per run would pass on six of seven
// and still read plausibly.
echo Runs::$agreed, " capacities agree, ", Runs::$disagreed, " do not", "\n";
--EXPECT--
7 capacities agree, 0 do not
